From fb7b4b8a5af7c37348bd9d87ec8b5e4e129844fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Martin Mirchev <mmirchev@comp.nus.edu.sg>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:34:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mprintf: reject a '*' width followed by digits

In a format like "%*128d" the digits overwrote the width while
FLAGS_WIDTHPARAM stayed set, so parsefmt() used the number as an
argument index and checked the usedinput bitmap with it before the
MAX_PARAMETERS check. That read past the bitmap, and "%*2147483647d"
crashed.

Return an error for such a format, as for other invalid widths.

Extend test 557.

Closes #24029

Upstream: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/fb7b4b8a5af7c37348bd9d87ec8b5e4e129844fc
---
 lib/mprintf.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/mprintf.c b/lib/mprintf.c
index f5b7282..e86b8db 100644
--- a/lib/mprintf.c
+++ b/lib/mprintf.c
@@ -307,6 +307,9 @@ static int parse_flags(const char **fmtp, unsigned int *flagsp, int use_dollar,
     case '8':
     case '9': {
       curl_off_t num;
+      if(flags & FLAGS_WIDTHPARAM)
+        /* a width cannot be both '*' and a number */
+        return PFMT_WIDTH;
       flags |= FLAGS_WIDTH;
       fmt--;
       if(curlx_str_number(&fmt, &num, INT_MAX))
